## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly multi-tenant flow with prompt catalog versioning, failover, and privacy controls in Azure OpenAI.

## Key Concepts

- API Management routing with tenant context
- Versioned prompt catalog stored in Azure Blob
- Per-tenant quotas and graceful fallback
- PII redaction via Text Analytics before OpenAI calls
- Regional OpenAI endpoints and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
async function routeRequest(tenantId, payload) {
  const tmpl = await blob.get(`prompts/${tenantId}/v1.json`);
  const redacted = pii.redact(payload.prompt, payload.user);
  const finalPrompt = `${tmpl.promptPrefix}\n${redacted}`;
  return openai.chat(finalPrompt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test failover if the catalog fetch fails?
- How would you monitor per-tenant quotas and trigger alerts?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess system design, data governance, and production-readiness for cross-tenant AI in security contexts.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant RBAC, per-tenant quotas
- KB-grounded prompts via Cognitive Search
- PII/PHI redaction with Text Analytics
- Canary rollouts behind API Management
- Data residency, auditing, and cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-routing: validate tenant, fetch KB version, route to OpenAI with context
async function handleRequest(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const policy = await getTenantPolicy(tenant);
  const kb = await fetchKBVersion(tenant);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(req.body, kb, policy);
  return callOpenAI(prompt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-tenant audit logging and tamper-evidence in Data Lake?
- What tests would you run to verify canary prompt rollouts do not regress SLAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates practical API boundary design, basic rate limiting, and graceful degradation in a beginner-friendly Azure setup.

## Key Concepts
- API Management quotas and rate limits by user
- Per-user spend cap and simple state store
- Fallback to rule-based replies on failure
- Observability for quotas and failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal mock of request flow with quota check and fallback
async function handle(req) {
  const user = req.userId;
  const quota = getUserQuota(user);
  if (quota.remaining <= 0) return cannedReply();
  try {
    const res = await callOpenAI(req.payload);
    debitQuota(user, req.payload.length);
    return res;
  } catch {
    return cannedReply();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota expiry and fallback paths?
- How would you simulate OpenAI downtime in a local test environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Understands multi-tenant data separation, policy gates, and regulated data handling in Azure OpenAI stacks.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant RBAC with tenant-scoped keys in Key Vault
- PII redaction and data consent gating
- Grounding via Cognitive Search KB + versioning
- Canary deployment and human handoff
- Auditable logging to Data Lake

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: redact PII using Azure Text Analytics before prompt submission
async function redactPII(input, client) {
  const res = await client.recognizePII(input, { language: 'en' });
  // replace found PII spans with [REDACTED]
  return performRedaction(input, res);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test per-tenant data isolation and key rotation?
- How to set thresholds and automate handoff without degrading SLAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect a multi-tenant Azure OpenAI flow integrated with DevOps, covering data lineage, security, and performance.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI + API Management
- Cognitive Search KB in Blob
- Per-tenant RBAC (Azure AD)
- PII redaction (Text Analytics)
- Data Lake logging
- Canary deployments and end-to-end latency goals

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestration snippet showing tenant context, diff->prompt, and moderation
async function reviewPR(prDiff, tenantId){
  const kb = await loadKB(tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(prDiff, kb, tenantId);
  const review = await openAiReview(prompt);
  if(hasPIIRisk(review)){ redact(review); }
  await storeLogs(tenantId, {prDiff, review});
  return review.comments;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate and monitor prompt drift across tenants without leaking data?
- What latency and cost controls would you put in place for high-velocity repos?
